    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: This is an Outstanding Offer\n\n**This is an exceptional, top-of-market (top 5\u201310%) offer for an L6 Product Manager at Google in the Bay Area.** \n\nGoogle has already treated you as a tier-1 hire. Base salary is near the absolute top of the listed band, the RSU grant is well above the median for L6, and the $200,000 sign-on bonus is far higher than typical initial offers.\n\n---\n\n### Compensation Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### 1. Year-by-Year Realized Compensation\nGoogle typically uses a **front-loaded GSU vesting schedule** (commonly 38% in Year 1, 32% in Year 2, 20% in Year 3, and 10% in Year 4, or 33%/33%/22%/12%):\n\n| Component | Your Meta Current (IC6) | Google L6 (Year 1) | Google L6 (Year 2) | Google L6 (Year 3\u20134 Avg)* |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 | $260,000 |\n| **Bonus (Target)** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) | $52,000 (20%) |\n| **Equity (RSUs)** | ~$250,000 | ~$445k\u2013$509k (33\u201338%) | ~$428k (32%) | ~$201k + Refreshes |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | \u2014 | $200,000 | \u2014 | \u2014 |\n| **Total Realized** | **~$526,000** | **~$957,000\u2013$1,021,000** | **~$740,000** | **~$513,000 + Refreshes** |\n\n*\\*Note: Google's front-loading means Year 1 and Year 2 are heavily amplified. By Years 3 and 4, your annualized new-hire grant drops, which is intended to be offset by annual performance refreshers stacking on top of each other.*\n\n#### 2. How the Components Benchmark at L6 (Bay Area)\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** Top of band. The job posting cap is $271,000; being offered $260k leaves almost no headroom to negotiate base.\n* **Equity ($1,340,000 over 4 yrs / ~$335k/yr avg):** The median L6 PM equity grant is ~$800k\u2013$1M over 4 years ($200k\u2013$250k/yr). A $1.34M grant is near the 90th+ percentile.\n* **Sign-on ($200,000):** Standard L6 sign-on bonuses typically range between $50,000 and $100,000. $200,000 was calibrated specifically to offset your unvested Meta equity ($200k over 12 months) and close you without hesitation.\n\n---\n\n### Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** **No.** Leaving Meta for this role represents a massive step up in compensation and career trajectory.\n* **Aggressive Negotiation?** **Not recommended.** You do not have competing leverage, Google is your first choice, and they already gave you a near-ceiling package. Aggressive counter-demands could test goodwill or trigger internal compensation committee friction without meaningful upside.\n* **Recommendation:** **Accept, with an optional \"soft close\" ask.** \n\nBecause the offer is already at top-tier calibration, you have two smart paths:\n\n---\n\n### Option A: Accept Directly (Safest & Strongest Relationship Builder)\nTell the recruiter that you reviewed the offer, appreciate that they came in with a top-of-market package as promised, and you are thrilled to sign. This builds maximum goodwill with the hiring manager and team from Day 1.\n\n---\n\n### Option B: A Low-Risk \"One-Touch\" Close Ask\nIf you want to leave no money on the table, you can make a single, polite request framed around **closing immediately**. \n\nDo not ask for a higher base salary. The only areas with marginal flexibility are **equity** or a **slight sign-on increase**.\n\n#### What to Ask For:\n* Ask to round the equity grant up to **$1,450,000** (or $1,500,000) over 4 years.\n\n#### How to Pitch It (Email or Phone Script):\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter],*  \n>  \n> *Thank you so much for putting this package together. I'm genuinely thrilled about the team and the scope of this L6 role, and Google is unequivocally my first choice.*  \n>  \n> *The offer is very strong. If we can get the new-hire equity to **$1,450,000**, I am ready to sign the offer letter today without hesitation. Either way, I'm very excited about joining the team\u2014let me know if there's any flexibility on that number.\"*\n\n#### Why this works:\n1. **It presents no risk:** You explicitly state that Google is your top choice and you are committed.\n2. **It gives the recruiter a mandate:** Recruiters can take a \"if you give X, they will sign today\" proposal directly to comp approval.\n3. **If they say no:** You gracefully accept the original $1.34M / $200k sign-on package without any awkwardness.",
